'''''Schefflera digitata''''', commonly known as '''patē, seven finger''' (or '''seven-finger'''), is a species of tree in the family [[Araliaceae]]. It is [[endemic]] to New Zealand and is widespread in the [[North Island|North]], [[South Island|South]], and [[Stewart Island]]s. It occurs in lowland to montane forests from sea level to {{convert|1200|m|abbr=on}}. It prefers damp, shady parts of the forest and is common along stream banks. It is the only New Zealand member of the genus ''[[Schefflera]]'', which has 12 other species in Oceania.

''Schefflera digitata'' usually grows to heights of {{convert|4–6|m|abbr=off}}. The species is often confused with [[Pseudopanax arboreus|five-finger]] (''Pseudopanax arboreus''). ''S. digitata'' was first [[Species description|described]] in 1776 by the German naturalists [[Georg Forster|Georg]] and [[Johann Reinhold Forster]]. ''S. digitata'' is pollinated by insects. The fruits are [[Biological dispersal|dispersed]] by [[Frugivore|fruit-eating animals]] (frugivores), such as birds. ''S. digitata''{{'}}s timber was utilised by indigenous [[Māori people|Māori]] to obtain fire by friction. The juice of the fruits was used as a dye by Māori, and [[European settlers in New Zealand|European settlers]] used it as a substitute for ink. ''S. digitata''{{'}}s 2023 [[conservation status]] in the [[New Zealand Threat Classification System]] was "Not Threatened".

==Description==
[[File:Schefflera digitata - John Barkla - 214303192.jpeg|thumb|right|alt=Small hanging purple fruits of S. digitata|Small hanging fruits of ''S. digitata'']]

''Schefflera digitata'' (patē) is an [[evergreen]] and [[dioecious]] species of tree in the family [[Araliaceae]]. It reaches a height of up to {{convert|8–9|m|abbr=off}} tall,{{Sfn|De Lange|2026}}{{Sfn|Burrows|1995|p=3}} although more commonly {{convert|4–6|m|abbr=on}} tall.{{Sfn|Wardle|2011|p=249}} The [[Trunk (botany)|trunk]] is branched and the [[Bark (botany)|bark]] is greenish in colour and ridged with scattered bumps. Its reddish, round [[Petiole (botany)|petiole]]s are up to {{convert|25|cm|abbr=on}} long. Its [[petiolule]]s are also reddish and are up to {{convert|2|cm|abbr=on}} long.{{Sfn|De Lange|2026}}

Leaves are palmately compound, usually with seven leaflets (called 'fingers'); mature leaves are green above and paler below, while young leaves are shiny and purplish underneath. The terminal leaflets are the largest, up to {{convert|20|cm|abbr=on}} long, with smaller side leaflets. The leaflets are broader toward the tip, pointed to blunt at the ends. The leaf margins are sharply toothed in adults and irregularly lobed in young plants. The leaves are similar, and are often confused with [[Pseudopanax arboreus|five-finger]] (''Pseudopanax arboreus''); although the leaflets of ''P. aboreus'' are smaller, thicker and have larger teeth.{{Sfn|De Lange|2026}}{{Sfn|Dawson|Lucas|2011|p=392}}

The [[inflorescence]]s (flower clusters) are found on hanging [[panicle]]s, with numerous spreading branches which are up to {{convert|35|cm|abbr=on}} long.{{Sfn|Salmon|1986|p=248}}{{Sfn|De Lange|2026}} The [[bract]]s and [[bracteole]]s are small. Each [[umbel]] contains up to ten flowers. The [[Peduncle (botany)|peduncle]]s are {{convert|10|mm|abbr=on}} long, but the [[Pedicel (botany)|pedicel]]s are shorter. Flowers are small, at about {{convert|7|mm|abbr=on}} in diameter and greenish-cream in colour. The [[petal]]s are usually {{convert|5|mm|abbr=on}} in diameter. Each flower has five filamentous [[stamen]]s. Fruits are  dark purple or violet when ripened, somewhat globe-shaped, and about {{convert|3.5|mm|abbr=on}} in diameter. The seeds are {{convert|2–2.5|mm|abbr=on}} long.{{Sfn|De Lange|2026}}{{sfn|Lee|Hodgkinson|Johnson|1990|p=2}}

==Taxonomy==
''Schefflera digitata'' was first [[Species description|described]] in 1776 by the German naturalists [[Georg Forster|Georg]] and [[Johann Reinhold Forster]] in their book ''[[Characteres generum plantarum]]'', which was made during the [[second voyage of James Cook]].{{Sfn|IPNI|2026}}{{Sfn|Forster|Forster|1776}} ''S. digitata'' is the [[type species]] of the genus ''[[Schefflera]]''.{{sfn|Plunkett|Lowry|Frodin|Wen|2005|p=2}} It is the only New Zealand member of the genus ''Schefflera''.{{Sfn|POWO|2026}}{{Sfn|Laing|1906|p=312}} There are thirteen species of this genus currently accepted by the [[Plants of the World Online]] taxonomic database. These species are native to islands in the Pacific: New Zealand, Fiji, New Caledonia, Samoa and Vanuatu.{{Sfn|POWO|2026}}

===Etymology===
The [[etymology]] (word origin) of ''S. digitata''{{'}}s genus name, ''Schefflera'', is named in honour of the physician {{Interlanguage link|Johann Peter Ernst von Scheffler|de}}.{{Sfn|Gledhill|2008|p=343}} The [[specific epithet]] (second part of the scientific name), ''digitata'', means 'divided into fingers' or 'finger-like parts'. It is derived from the [[Latin]] {{lang|la|digitus}} meaning 'finger'.{{Sfn|Eagle|2006|p=476}} The species is [[Common name|commonly known]] as 'patē' and 'seven-finger'.{{Sfn|De Lange|2026}}

==Ecology==
''Schefflera digitata''{{'}}s seeds are [[Biological dispersal|dispersed]] by [[Frugivore|fruit-eating animals]] such as birds.{{Sfn|Burrows|1995|p=3}} Several birds have been recorded as seed dispersers, including [[New Zealand bellbird|bellbirds]] (''Anthornis melanura''), [[kererū]] (''Hemiphaga novaeseelandiae''), [[kōkako]] (''Callaeas''), [[tūī]] (''Prosthemadera novaeseelandiae''), [[stitchbird]]s (''Notiomystis cincta''), and [[silvereye]]s (''Zosterops lateralis'').{{Sfn|Crowe|2009|p=10}} [[Pollination]] is achieved by insects.{{Sfn|Dawson|Lucas|2011|p=392}} The caterpillars of the [[Meterana merope|patē owlet]] (''Meterana merope'') feed on the tree.{{Sfn|Crowe|2009|p=10}} ''S. digitata'' also plays [[Host (biology)|host]] to the [[Glaucias amyoti|New Zealand vegetable bug]] (''Glaucias amyoti'') and [[Oemona hirta|lemon tree borer]] (''Oemona hirta'').{{Sfn|Sopow|Bain|2017|pp=10–11}} ''S. digitata'' is also a primary host of the root parasite, ''[[Dactylanthus taylorii]]''.{{Sfn|Wardle|2011|p=250}}

==Distribution==
''Schefflera digitata'' is [[endemic]] to New Zealand. It is widespread in the [[North Island|North]], [[South Island|South]], and [[Stewart Island]]s.{{Sfn|De Lange|2026}} The [[conservation status]] of ''S. digitata'' in the [[New Zealand Threat Classification System]] was evaluated in 2023 as "Not Threatened".{{Sfn|De Lange|2026}}

===Habitat===
''Schefflera digitata'' is typically found in lowland to [[montane]] forests, growing from sea level up to {{convert|1,200|m|abbr=on}} above sea level at maximum elevation.{{Sfn|Salmon|1986|p=248}}{{Sfn|Wardle|2011|p=249}} It is a [[Shade tolerance|shade-tolerant]] species, and it prefers well-drained and fertile soils, commonly establishing itself along streams in shaded areas.{{Sfn|Wardle|2011|p=249}} ''S. digitata'' can also establish itself as an [[epiphyte]] (growing on another plant) and in wet rocky areas.{{Sfn|Wardle|2011|pp=249–250}}

==Uses==
''Schefflera digitata''{{'}}s timber was used by indigenous [[Māori people|Māori]] to obtain fire by friction. There are no recorded uses of the timber by [[European settlers in New Zealand|European settlers]]. The juice of the fruits was used as a dye by Māori, and Europeans used it as a substitute for ink.{{Sfn|Wardle|2011|p=249}} ''S. digitata''{{'}}s [[Māori language|Māori]] names include {{lang|mi|patē}}, {{lang|mi|patatē}}, {{lang|mi|kōtētē}}, and {{lang|mi|kohi}}.{{Sfn|Manaaki Whenua|2023}}

''Schefflera digitata'' had medicinal significance to Māori as well. ''S. digitata''{{'}}s leaves contain compounds of [[falcarindiol]], which may be beneficial in treating skin fungus. The sap of the tree was used to cure various skin diseases.{{Sfn|Crowe|2009|p=10}}{{Sfn|Manaaki Whenua|2023}} The leaves were also used to wrap newborn Māori babies to keep them warm until they began to perspire.{{Sfn|Wardle|2011|p=249}}{{Sfn|Crowe|2009|p=10}}

